latest_plan = "Based on the reflection and the patterns observed, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Scan the input grid:\n   a. Identify all non-zero (colored) cells.\n   b. Store their positions (row, column) and colors.\n\n2. Find the two horizontally closest colored dots:\n   a. Calculate the horizontal distance between each pair of colored dots.\n   b. Select the pair with the smallest horizontal distance.\n\n3. Determine the diamond shape:\n   a. Let's call the left dot A and the right dot B.\n   b. Calculate the midpoint between A and B (mid_row, mid_col).\n   c. Determine the height of the diamond:\n      - Calculate the vertical distance between A and B.\n      - Set the diamond height to about half this distance (rounded down).\n   d. Calculate the positions of the four points of the diamond:\n      - Top: (mid_row - height/2, A's column)\n      - Bottom: (mid_row + height/2, B's column)\n      - Left: (mid_row, mid_col - height/2)\n      - Right: (mid_row, mid_col + height/2)\n\n4. Color the diamond:\n   a. Top point: Use the color of dot A.\n   b. Bottom point: Use the color of dot B.\n   c. Left point: Use Gray (5).\n   d. Right point: Calculate (A's color + B's color) % 10.\n\n5. Draw the diamond:\n   a. Create a new grid identical to the input grid.\n   b. Color the four points of the diamond as determined in step 4.\n   c. Draw lines connecting these points:\n      - From top to left and right.\n      - From bottom to left and right.\n   d. Use the color of the endpoint closer to A for the top-left and bottom-left lines.\n   e. Use the color of the endpoint closer to B for the top-right and bottom-right lines.\n\n6. Return the new grid with the added diamond shape.\n\nImplementation notes:\n- When drawing lines, use a line drawing algorithm (e.g., Bresenham's line algorithm) to determine which cells to color.\n- Ensure that all calculations for positions are rounded to integers and stay within the grid boundaries.\n- If the calculated diamond shape would extend beyond the grid boundaries, adjust it to fit within the grid.\n\nThis plan should work for the given examples and should generalize well to other cases, including those with only two dots or with the dots in different configurations. It focuses on the two horizontally closest dots and creates a consistent diamond shape between them, which aligns with the patterns observed in the reflection."
